 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method requires computing blending weights, losses, and gradients for all Gaussians and views, which is $O(N \cdot |V|)$. Sorting and scoring are $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$. The relocation step is $O(M)$ where $M \ll N$. Overall, it adds a periodic overhead to the training loop [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing statistics ($C^{mean}, C^{max}, G^{mean}, C^{HE}$) for all Gaussians, which is $O(N)$ [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:** Experiments on MS-GS with three real-scene benchmarks demonstrate improved multi-scale rendering quality, particularly at coarse scales [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** It redirects primitive allocation toward persistent coarse-scale errors without increasing the primitive count. It uses adaptive relocation count, parameter reset, and continued optimization as a stabilization strategy, avoiding explicit rollback or separate re-correction modules [S1].
